// Single expression — no braces, no `return`, the value after => is returned automatically.
const square = (n: number) => n * n;
console.log(square(5));

// Block body — braces mean you write `return` yourself, same as a normal function.
const greet = (name: string) => {
  const message = `hello, ${name}`;
  return message;
};
console.log(greet("Leela"));

// A stand-in for something slow — a real Playwright action works the same way underneath.
const wait = (ms: number) => new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, ms));

const slowGreet = async (name: string) => {
  await wait(200);                      // pauses here, doesn't block anything else
  return `hello (after a pause), ${name}`;
};

console.log(await slowGreet("Katy"));   // top-level await, only works in a notebook cell

const describeUser = ({ name, email }: { name: string; email: string }) =>
  `${name} <${email}>`;

console.log(describeUser({ name: "Leela", email: "leela@gmail.com" }));

// This is the exact shape Playwright uses to hand a test its tools.
const showFixtures = ({ page, browserName }: { page: string; browserName: string }) =>
  `page=${page}, browser=${browserName}`;

console.log(showFixtures({ page: "a Page object", browserName: "chromium" }));

**Expected result:** `Leela <leela@gmail.com>`, then `page=a Page object, browser=chromium`.

### Where you'll see this in Playwright code

Every single test you write in this course starts like this:

```ts
test("some name", async ({ page }) => {
  // page is destructured straight out of the object Playwright hands in
});
```

`async` because the body will `await` Playwright actions. `({ page })` because destructuring
pulls just the `page` fixture out of everything Playwright could offer. You'll type this exact
shape in [Fundamentals](/learn/w1/d5/p2).

## Anatomy of a Playwright test

```ts
import { test, expect } from "@playwright/test";

test("a name describing what this proves", async ({ page }) => {
  // steps, using `page`
});
```

| Piece | What it is |
|---|---|
| `import { test, expect } from "@playwright/test"` | Named imports — the two functions every spec needs ([TypeScript](/learn/w1/d5/p1), section 4) |
| `test("name", fn)` | Registers one test; the name is what shows up in the report |
| `async ({ page }) => {}` | The test body — `page` is destructured out of Playwright's fixtures ([TypeScript](/learn/w1/d5/p1), section 3) |
| `page` | A real, isolated browser tab — every action on it returns a Promise, so you `await` it |

That's the whole shape. Everything else in this course is what you put *inside* the function.

