`week1/generated/day5-practice-login.spec.ts`:

```ts
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('Katy can sign in', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('https://test-automation-banking.vercel.app/');
  await page.getByPlaceholder('Enter Email').fill('katy@gmail.com');
  await page.getByPlaceholder('Enter Password').fill('Katy@12354');
  await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Login' }).click();

  // The assertion is the test. Without it this passes on a wrong password.
  await expect(page).toHaveURL(/dashboard/);
  await expect(page.getByText('Katy')).toBeVisible();
});
```

```bash
npx playwright test week1/generated/day5-practice-login.spec.ts
```

**Now do the part that matters.** Change the password to something wrong and run it again. You want `1 failed` — and you want to read *how* it failed: `expect(page).toHaveURL` timed out waiting for the URL to match, because the app never left the login screen.

A test you have never seen fail is a test you have no reason to trust. Proving it can fail costs one run.

> `getByText('Katy')` without `exact: true` matches the greeting whether it renders as `Katy` or `Welcome, Katy` — deliberate, and worth keeping.

**This is a two-run exercise — the value is in the difference between them.**

`week1/generated/day5-practice-gap.spec.ts`, run 1 — no assertion at all:

```ts
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('login with a wrong password', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('https://test-automation-banking.vercel.app/');
  await page.getByPlaceholder('Enter Email').fill('leela@gmail.com');
  await page.getByPlaceholder('Enter Password').fill('definitely-wrong');
  await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Login' }).click();
  // Nothing asserted. Every line above "worked".
});
```

`npx playwright test week1/generated/day5-practice-gap.spec.ts` → **`1 passed`**, in green, on a login that did not happen.

Now add one line to the *same* test and re-run:

```ts
  await expect(page).toHaveURL(/dashboard/);
```

→ **`1 failed`**.

**One sentence on what changed:**

> Nothing changed about the application or the steps. The login failed identically both times. What changed is that the second run *checked* the outcome. The report went from a green tick meaning "no line threw" to a red failure meaning "the user did not get in".

**The lesson.** A Playwright action only asserts that it could *perform* the action. `fill` succeeded, `click` succeeded — neither has any opinion about whether logging in worked. Without `expect`, a suite can be fully green while the product is broken. That is the assertions gap, and it is the most expensive habit to acquire late.

Two files. The class owns every locator; the test owns none.

`week1/generated/login-page.ts`:

```ts
import { type Page } from '@playwright/test';

export class LoginPage {
  constructor(private readonly page: Page) {}

  async signIn(email: string, password: string): Promise<void> {
    await this.page.goto('https://test-automation-banking.vercel.app/');
    await this.page.getByPlaceholder('Enter Email').fill(email);
    await this.page.getByPlaceholder('Enter Password').fill(password);
    await this.page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Login' }).click();
  }

  async signedInName(name: string): Promise<string> {
    // No exact: true - the greeting may render as "Welcome, Leela".
    return (await this.page.getByText(name).innerText()).trim();
  }
}
```

`week1/generated/day5-practice-pom.spec.ts`:

```ts
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';
import { LoginPage } from './login-page';

test('the signed-in user is the one who signed in', async ({ page }) => {
  const login = new LoginPage(page);
  await login.signIn('leela@gmail.com', 'Leela@123');

  const greeting = await login.signedInName('Leela');
  expect(greeting).toContain('Leela');
});
```

**Two things to check in your own version.**

**No locator appears in the spec.** Not one `getByRole`, not one CSS selector. If the app's login markup changes, exactly one file changes — and it is not the test.

**The assertion stayed in the test.** `signedInName` *returns* a value; it does not assert on it. That is the rule the course holds to throughout: page objects do things and report what they found, tests decide whether that is right. A class that asserts internally is a class you cannot reuse in a test that expects the other outcome.

> The filename matters: `login-page.ts`, **not** `login-page.spec.ts`. Give a helper a spec suffix and the runner will try to execute it as a test file — the trap you proved on [Day 4 - Implementation](/learn/w1/d4/p3).
>
> This is a page object in all but name. Week 5 names it.
